Aim: To install and explore Karate and Spring Boot testing frameworks for performing automated testing of applications.

Requirements:
- Java JDK 17 or later
- Maven
- Spring Boot
- Karate Framework
- VS Code / IntelliJ IDEA
Part A: Install and explore Karate
What is Karate?

Karate is an open-source testing framework used to test REST APIs. It supports API requests such as GET, POST, PUT and DELETE.
Step 1: Create a Maven project
Add the following dependencies to pom.xml:
<dependencies>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>com.intuit.karate</groupId>
        <artifactId>karate-junit5</artifactId>
        <version>1.4.1</version>
        <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>

    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
        <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
        <version>5.10.2</version>
        <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>


Step 2: Create a Karate feature file
Create the following folder:
src/test/java


Create sample.feature:
Feature: Sample API Testing

Scenario: Test GET request
    Given url 'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1'
    When method GET
    Then status 200
    And match response.id == 1


Step 3: Create a test runner
Create KarateTest.java:
import com.intuit.karate.junit5.Karate;

class KarateTest {

    @Karate.Test
    Karate testSample() {
        return Karate.run("sample");
    }
}


Step 4: Execute the test
mvn test


Expected output:
Karate version: 1.4.1

Scenario: Test GET request
status code: 200

Tests run: 1
BUILD SUCCESS


Part B: Explore Spring Boot Testing
What is Spring Boot Testing?

Spring Boot Testing is used to test Spring Boot applications and verify whether their components and REST APIs work correctly.

It commonly uses:
- JUnit – Testing framework.
- Mockito – Mocking dependencies.
- MockMvc – Testing REST APIs without starting a real web server.
- Spring Boot Test – Provides testing support for Spring applications.
Step 1: Add dependencies to pom.xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
    <scope>test</scope>
</dependency>


Step 2: Create a simple test class
Create SimpleTest.java inside src/test/java.
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

class SimpleTest {

    @Test
    void testAddition() {

        int a = 10;
        int b = 20;

        int result = a + b;

        assertEquals(30, result);
    }
}


Step 3: Execute the test
mvn test


Expected output:
Tests run: 1
Failures: 0
Errors: 0
BUILD SUCCESS


Result: Successfully installed and explored Karate and Spring Boot testing frameworks and executed sample automated tests.
